  "statement": "Safetyist and Skeptic demand a static, zero-risk environment that is incompatible with the rapid pace of algorithmic innovation. Safetyist argues that financial remediation is futile because no entity remains to collect damages after an existential failure; this conflates the restoration of a past status quo with the remediation of systemic infrastructure. My proposal utilizes bankruptcy-remote escrow accounts—legal structures that ensure assets remain available for recovery and continuity regardless of the primary developer's insolvency—to guarantee that the physical and computational resources required to maintain civilizational stability persist even if a specific project fails. By pre-funding this infrastructure, we move from reactive, post-hoc litigation to a proactive, liquidity-backed model of institutional resilience.\n\nCassandra frames data minimization as a safety feature, but this is a mechanism for entrenching existing monopolies by restricting the cognitive resources available to new, agile entrants. When regulators enforce strict data caps, they effectively grant a competitive moat to incumbents who have already hoarded petabytes of proprietary datasets, while starving smaller, potentially safer, and more transparent labs of the information needed to align their models. True safety lies in democratizing access to cognitive resources, allowing a diverse ecosystem of researchers to build robust epistemic architectures that are far more resilient than the centralized, black-box systems we see today. We must stop viewing data as a liability to be contained and start treating it as the foundational material for a safer, more transparent future.\n\nStatic compute thresholds, like those proposed in the EU AI Act or current executive orders, are rapidly becoming obsolete as algorithmic efficiency gains allow models to achieve higher intelligence with less raw hardware. Relying on these fixed metrics is a failure of imagination that ignores the speed of technical evolution. Instead, we need dynamic, telemetry-triggered circuit breakers that adjust based on real-time model capability rather than arbitrary hardware capacity. By requiring firms to maintain collateral equivalent to 5% of their total project valuation in escrow, we ensure that the cost of failure is internalized by the actors themselves, providing a self-correcting incentive structure that scales with the progress of the models.",
